  "summary": "The health-tech landscape today reflects several interconnected pressures shaping the industry. Health plan members are experiencing stagnant satisfaction levels as rising costs continue to burden consumers, while operational inefficiencies—from small operating room scheduling misses to diagnostic and identity data gaps—are creating hidden financial drains across healthcare systems. Technology vendors are being challenged to rethink engagement strategies, with buyers increasingly wanting to lead conversations rather than passively consume pitches, while healthcare organizations grapple with cybersecurity threats, the responsible deployment of AI systems, and optimizing language models for clinical accuracy. Three bright spots emerge: Samsung and Abbott's collaboration bringing extended reality to blood donation sites, research demonstrating that online tools can reverse frailty in seniors, and growing recognition that human-centered design—including sensory-regulated care environments and reliable transportation access—matters alongside clinical efficiency. European healthcare systems are exploring ways to break down traditional borders, though the industry remains fundamentally divided on basic terminology, with readers split on whether it's \"health care\" or \"healthcare.\"",
